1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. Here's a problem that surprises a lot of developers. You send the exact same prompt

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
twice and get two different answers, and you can't just dial that away with a setting anymore. This

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
lesson covers sampling, why output varies, and what actually changed on current models. Let's

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
break it down. Let's start with three terms. Sampling is the process of selecting each next

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
token from the model's output probability distribution. That distribution is the model's

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
ranked set of guesses for what comes next, each with a likelihood attached, and the sampler draws

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
from it. And non-determinism is the property that the same prompt can yield different outputs across

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
different calls. Those three ideas are tied together. Because generation samples from a

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
distribution, rather than always taking the single top guess, the output isn't guaranteed

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
to be byte identical from one run to the next. Get comfortable with that, because it shapes how

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
you design everything downstream of the model. On older models, you had three knobs to shape how

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
that next token got picked. Temperature was the main one. Turn it up, and the odds flattened,

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
so lower ranked tokens had a better chance, which felt more creative. Turn it down towards zero,

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
and the odds sharpened toward the single most likely token. Then top p kept only the most

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:31,000
probable tokens up to a cumulative probability threshold, and sampled from that set. And top k

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
simply capped the candidate pool to a fixed number of top tokens. Together, these three let you trade

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
predictability against variety. That was the classic mental model for controlling randomness,

18
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
and for years, it's how everyone tuned this. Here's the shift you need to know. On current

19
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
models, those sampling parameters are gone. Passing temperature, top p, or top k returns an

20
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
HTTP 400 error. This isn't a case where the model quietly ignores them. It rejects the request

21
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:12,000
outright. And this applies to the current tier, Fable 5, Opus 5, Opus 4.8, and Sonnet 5. One

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
nuance on Sonnet 5, the default value is still accepted. Only non-default values trigger the 400.

23
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
So if you've got old code that sets temperature to 0.7 out of habit, it'll break against these

24
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
models. The replacement isn't another knob. You steer behavior through clearer prompting,

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
and through the effort setting. Tighten the instruction, show the format you want, and let

26
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
the model's own reasoning do the shaping. Let's put old and new side by side, because the

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,000
difference matters when you migrate. The legacy approach adjusted randomness directly with

28
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
temperature, top p, and top k. Those knobs are still valid on older tiers, like Sonnet 4.5,

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Haiku 4.5, and earlier. On the Clod 4 models, you could even pass one of temperature or top p,

30
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
but never both at once. The current approach replaces all of that. Prompting and effort

31
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
are your levers now. And the old parameters aren't just ignored, they're rejected. The practical rule

32
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
is simple. Name your target model first, and then you know whether sampling knobs are even an option.

33
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
On the frontier models, they aren't. Now to the part that unsettles people. Even with identical

34
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
inputs, a model can return different responses. Why? At its core, next token selection is

35
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
probabilistic, so the model can land on a slightly different path from one call to the next.

36
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
On top of that, serving conditions can shift behavior. The request might route differently,

37
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
or safety classifiers and sampling logic on the serving side can change, all without any change

38
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
to the model's weights. The weights are fixed for a given model, and yet the output you observe can

39
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,000
still vary. So the honest engineering stance is this. Never assume two calls return the exact

40
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
same string, even when everything you control is the same. This brings us to the single most common

41
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
misconception here, the temperature zero trap. The myth goes like this. Set temperature to zero,

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
and you'll get fully deterministic, perfectly repeatable output every time. It sounds reasonable,

43
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
and it isn't true. Even on the older models that accepted it, temperature zero never actually

44
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
guaranteed identical outputs. It made things more deterministic, not perfectly so. And on

45
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,000
current models, the parameter is rejected outright anyway, so you can't even set it.

46
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
If your pipeline is leaning on temperature zero as a guarantee of repeatability,

47
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
that guarantee was never really there. Design as if the output can vary, because it can.

48
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Let's name where the variation actually comes from, because it's more than one source. First,

49
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
probabilistic sampling. Each token is drawn from a distribution, so a lower ranked token can surface

50
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:06,000
now and then. Second, serving infrastructure. Routers, safety classifiers, and sampling logic

51
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
all live between you and the weights, and any of them can nudge behavior. Third, and this is the

52
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
subtle one, fixed weights don't mean fixed output. You can pin a model ID so the weights never change,

53
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
and still see the observable output drift over time. Put those together, and you stop expecting

54
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
a frozen, repeatable string, and start expecting a stable meaning with a variable surface.

55
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
So how do you build for this? You design pipelines that expect variation instead of fighting it.

56
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
The core habit is to validate and parse every response defensively. Don't write code that

57
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
compares the model's output against one exact, byte-identical string you're hoping for,

58
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
because that test will eventually fail on a run that's perfectly correct, but worded differently.

59
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Where an exact format genuinely matters, reach for structured outputs, add a validation step,

60
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and build in a retry if the parse fails. Treat the model like a component whose output shape can

61
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
shift slightly between calls, and guard the boundary where its text enters your system.

62
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
That defensiveness is what makes a pipeline reliable.

63
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Here's the bottom line. Sampling picks each token from a probability distribution, so output isn't

64
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
guaranteed identical. The legacy knobs were temperature, top p, and top k. On current models,

65
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:40,000
passing them returns an error. On one thing to watch, temperature zero was never fully

66
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
deterministic, so validate and parse defensively, rather than expecting one exact string.

67
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:53,000
That covers sampling, and why output varies in these models. Join me in the next one.

